lesson: 175
title: Two circles: both and neither
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Draw two overlapping circles. The children who like both things stand in the middle part, where the circles meet.
explain: If we add the two groups, the children in the middle are counted twice, so we take them away once.
explain: 30 children: 14 like football, 12 like chess, 5 like both. At least one: 14 + 12 - 5 = 21, neither: 30 - 21 = 9.
vars: n=15..40 a=5..30 b=5..30 x=2..12
need: x < a && x < b
need: a + b - x < n
answer: n - (a + b - x)
exercise: There are {n} children in a class. {a} like football, {b} like chess, and {x} like both. How many children like neither football nor chess?
exercise: In a group of {n} pupils, {a} have a dog, {b} have a cat, and {x} have both a dog and a cat. How many pupils have neither a dog nor a cat?
exercise: {n} children are at a camp. {a} go swimming, {b} go to the drawing club, and {x} do both. How many children do neither?
exercise: Of {n} pupils, {a} learn French and {b} learn German; {x} of them learn both languages. How many pupils learn neither of the two?
exercise: {n} people were asked about fruit. {a} like apples, {b} like bananas, {x} like both. How many people like neither apples nor bananas?
test: A survey of {n} children showed that {x} play both piano and guitar. In all, {a} play piano and {b} play guitar. How many children play no instrument of the two?
test: {b} of the {n} pupils in the class read comics and {a} read fairy tales; {x} pupils read both kinds. How many pupils read neither kind?
test: On a bus with {n} passengers, {a} have a bag, {b} have an umbrella, and {x} have a bag and an umbrella. How many passengers have neither?
test: {x} children in a class of {n} have both a brother and a sister. {a} children have a brother, {b} have a sister. How many children have no brother and no sister?
exercise: A class in Ohio has {n} pupils. {a} play soccer, {b} play basketball, and {x} play both sports. How many pupils play neither sport?
exercise: How many children like neither cats nor dogs, if in a group of {n} children {a} like cats, {b} like dogs and {x} like both?
exercise: Of {n} families on a street in Toronto, {a} own a car, {b} own a bike, and {x} own both. How many families own neither a car nor a bike?
exercise: {n} pupils: {a} take the bus, {b} bring a packed lunch, {x} do both. How many do neither?
exercise: At a summer camp in Ireland there are {n} children. {a} go hiking, {b} go canoeing, and {x} of them go hiking and canoeing too. How many children go neither hiking nor canoeing?
exercise: In a class of {n.words} pupils in Sydney, {a} went to the beach this summer, {b} went to the mountains, and {x} went to both. How many pupils went to neither?
exercise: {x} of the {n} guests at a party in Auckland drink both tea and coffee. Altogether {a} drink tea and {b} drink coffee. How many guests drink neither?
exercise: A shop in London had {n} customers. {a} bought bread, {b} bought milk, and {x} bought both bread and milk. How many customers bought neither?
test: In a club of {n} pupils in Canada, {a} can ski and {b} can skate; {x} can do both. How many pupils can do neither?
test: Not every one of the {n} runners in a race in Dublin wore a cap or gloves: {a} wore a cap, {b} wore gloves, and {x} wore both. How many runners wore neither?
test: {b} of the {n} houses in a village in Wales have a garden, {a} have a garage, and {x} have a garden and a garage. How many houses have neither?
test: In a vote of {n} children in Texas, {a} said yes to pizza, {b} said yes to tacos, and {x} said yes to both. How many children said yes to neither?
test: In a Year 4 class of {n} children in Melbourne, {a} have read the first book of a series, {b} have read the second, and {x} have read the two books. How many have read neither book?
test: Of {n} dogs at a show in Scotland, {a} have long hair, {b} have a black coat, and {x} have both. How many dogs have short hair and a coat that is not black?
exercise: {n} kids in class, {a} like footy, {b} like chess, {x} like both. how many like neither
exercise: {n} of us in the group — {a} got a dog, {b} a cat, {x} got both. how many got neither
exercise: class of {n}, {a} do swimming, {b} do piano, {x+1} do both — no sorry, {x}. how many do neither
exercise: Mia says it's {n} take away {a} take away {b}. nah, {x} of em do both. {n} kids, {a} like apples, {b} like pears, {x} like both — how many like neither
test: out of {n} pupils {a} read comics, {b} play video games, {x} do both. how many do neither of them things
test: {n} kids on the trip (3 teachers too but they dont count), {a} had sandwiches, {b} had pizza, {x} had both. how many had neither
test: {a} of the {n} went to the zoo, {b} went to the museum, {x} went to both. how many went to neither
exercise: In a class of {n} pupils, {a} have had a pet put to sleep, {b} have had a pet run away, and {x} have had both. How many pupils have had neither?
exercise: {n} of us in class. {a} got "easily distracted" on the report (talks too much), {b} got "needs to apply himself" (lazy), {x} got both lol. how many got neither, smart-arse
test: An office has {n} staff. {a} have been "invited for a chat with HR" (in trouble), {b} are "between projects" (no work to do), and {x} are both. How many staff are neither?
exam: Of a class comprising {n} children, it is recorded that {a} express a preference for football, {b} express a preference for chess, and {x} express a preference for both. Determine the number of children who express a preference for neither football nor chess.
exam: Within a cohort of {n} pupils, {a} are owners of a dog, {b} are owners of a cat, and {x} are owners of both a dog and a cat. Ascertain the number of pupils who are owners of neither animal.
exam: State the number of members of a group of {n} persons who belong to neither the choir nor the orchestra, given that {a} belong to the choir, {b} belong to the orchestra, and {x} belong to both bodies.
exam: It is given that, of {n} visitors to a library, {a} borrowed a book, {b} borrowed a magazine, and {x} borrowed items of both kinds. Determine the number of visitors who borrowed no item whatsoever.
